/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_LUT Lookup tables
 * @ingroup DspCore
 * @brief Move-only 1D, 2D and 3D arithmetic sample storage and sampling.
 *
 * Include `<DspCore_LUT.h>` or an individual `<DspCore/LUT/LUT1D.h>` header.
 * `DspCore::LUT::LUT1D<T>`, `LUT2D<T>` and `LUT3D<T>` store mutable arithmetic
 * types other than bool. Storage is independent of generation. When the
 * Interpolation module is also enabled, LUTs expose normalized sampled access.
 *
 * @section DspCore_LUT_Ownership Ownership and allocation
 *
 * Default construction is empty and does not allocate. Resize and Reserve
 * return false on overflow or allocation failure, preserving all existing
 * state. Reserve is an element count, not a byte count; it does not change
 * logical size. Tables own their contiguous storage through cpstd::vector,
 * so allocation follows the selected CPSTL backend. With CPSTL's native
 * backend, allocation failure leaves the vector unchanged and these methods
 * return false; with CPSTL_USING_STL, allocation follows std::vector's
 * configured behavior. Copy is disabled; moves transfer ownership without
 * allocating and leave the source dimensions empty.
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * DspCore::LUT::LUT1D<float> curve;
 * if (!curve.Reserve(64) || !curve.Resize(5)) { return; }
 * for (size_t x = 0; x < curve.Size(); ++x) {
 *     curve.SetValue(x, static_cast<float>(x) / 4.0f);
 * }
 * // curve.GetValue(4) == 1.0f
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section DspCore_LUT_Resize Dimensions and initialization
 *
 * Resize preserves the flat prefix. Newly exposed logical elements are zero,
 * including elements exposed after shrinking and growing within capacity.
 * Reshaping is not a geometric resampling operation. 2D uses y*width+x;
 * 3D uses (z*height+y)*width+x, with x contiguous.
 * Given extents @f$W@f$, @f$H@f$ and @f$D@f$, the row-major flat indices are
 * @f[
 * i_{1\mathrm D}(x)=x, \qquad
 * i_{2\mathrm D}(x,y)=yW+x, \qquad
 * i_{3\mathrm D}(x,y,z)=(zH+y)W+x.
 * @f]
 *
 * Any zero extent canonicalizes all extents and Size to zero while retaining
 * capacity and allocated storage. Release frees storage, clears the pointer,
 * dimensions and capacity, and is safe repeatedly. No virtual Eval is called.
 *
 * @section DspCore_LUT_Access Access and invalidation
 *
 * SetValue returns false without writing for invalid coordinates. TryGet
 * returns false without modifying its output. GetValue returns zero for an
 * invalid coordinate; use TryGet when zero is a meaningful stored value.
 * Fill changes logical elements only. GetBuffer exposes contiguous storage
 * for callers/generators; only the first Size elements may be accessed.
 * Const tables expose const pointers. Growing capacity and Release invalidate
 * previous pointers. Moves transfer the storage and its pointer to the new
 * owner. Shrinking or reshaping changes valid coordinates and element extent.
 * Reserve and allocation failures do not invalidate existing pointers.
 *
 * These owning resource types are not trivially copyable sample value types.
 * Do not resize/reserve/release in time-critical processing unless the caller
 * has explicitly budgeted that work. See @ref DspCore_Numeric.

 * @section DspCore_LUT_Sampling Normalized sampling
 *
 * `LUT::Sample(table, coordinates..., output, policy)` is an operation on a
 * LUT. It uses the generic Interpolation formulas internally, but lives in the
 * LUT module because it gives coordinates meaning relative to stored table
 * samples. It neither allocates nor changes the table; failure leaves @p output
 * unchanged.
 *
 * | Boundary | Domain and indexing |
 * | --- | --- |
 * | Clamp | Clamp to [0,1]; position=x*(N-1), with exact first/last endpoints |
 * | Reject | Same closed indexing, but outside [0,1] returns false |
 * | Wrap | Wrap cycles into [0,1); position=x*N, interpolating last-to-first |
 *
 * For a closed Clamp/Reject axis, @f$p=x(N-1)@f$. For Wrap, after wrapping
 * @f$x@f$ into @f$[0,1)@f$, @f$p=xN@f$. In each case the adjacent stored
 * samples are @f$\lfloor p\rfloor@f$ and its next valid index, and the local
 * interpolation coordinate is @f$t=p-\lfloor p\rfloor@f$.
 *
 * Wrap tables contain @f$N@f$ distinct periodic samples without a duplicate
 * endpoint. Thus a four-sample table has sample 1 at phase 0.25. Closed tables
 * include both endpoints; their sample 1 is at coordinate 1/3. Negative phase
 * and arbitrarily many finite cycles wrap with `fmod`.
 *
 * Empty tables, invalid policies and non-finite coordinates fail without
 * changing output. Singleton axes are constant for valid coordinates and
 * still obey Reject; no division by zero occurs. The output/coordinate type is
 * independent of table storage: integer samples are not PCM normalized, and a
 * narrowing storage conversion produces signed infinity when out of range.
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * DspCore::LUT::LUT1D<float> table;
 * if (!table.Resize(2)) { return; }
 * table.SetValue(0, 0.0f); table.SetValue(1, 10.0f);
 * float value = 0;
 * bool ok = DspCore::LUT::Sample(
 *     table, 0.25f, value, DspCore::LUT::Boundary::Clamp);
 * // value=2.5; no allocation during sampling.
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section DspCore_LUT_Examples Examples
 *
 * - LUT/Line: reserve, zero initialization, a normalized five-sample curve,
 *   shrink/regrowth and retained capacity.
 * - LUT/Surface: a row-major 3x2 surface, checked bounds and flat reshaping.
 * - LUT/Volume: a 2x3x4 volume, ownership transfer, overflow rejection and
 *   repeated release (regressions for the legacy 3D defects).
 * - LUT/Sampling: closed and periodic sampled access in 1D, 2D and 3D.
 *
 * All examples share their logic between Arduino and desktop and print
 * actual values, expected values and an overall PASS/FAIL result.
 */

/**
 * @namespace DspCore::LUT
 * @brief Owning arithmetic lookup-table storage in one, two and three dimensions.
 */
